@X03There are two basic methods of physically connecting to the Internet - dialup,
and digital full-time connections (T1, 56k, 128k, etc.)

@X0BFINDING A PROVIDER
------------------
@X03To connect to the Internet, you must find an Internet Service Provider (ISP).
This is a site somewhere which will let you connect to them, and they will
then connect you to the rest of the world.  There may be a problem finding a
provider in some areas.  If you need help finding a provider, then download
the file @X0CPROVIDER.ZIP@X03 from Salt Air.  This will tell you which providers are
available in your area code.  Once you have found a provider, you'll need to
decide which connectivity options you want.


@X0BDECIDING WHAT YOU WANT
----------------------
@X03Deciding what you want is important when connecting to an ISP.  Your ISP
will need to know what kind of connection you want, and the difference in
price between connections CAN vary greatly.


SLIP and PPP lines are available for DIAL-UP access, meaning that your
computer can call your provider through a modem adn a phone line and connect.
If you have just one or two users using a single SLIP or PPP dialup
connection, you'll be okay, but a modem connection support several users will
be divided by the number of users at the time, and can be bogged down very
quickly.


For a powerful and sturdy connection to the Internet, you may want a digital
line.  This is a dedicated full-time connection that usually uses
fiber-optics, and is WIRED stright into your house through your walls like a
phone line, and is done so by your provider.  The advantage to this connection
is that it is very fast, will support many users at a time, and will not
require negotiation like a modem will, and is much easier to connect.  Refused
connections and "DEAD" lines are usually the only cause for a missed
connection.  The single line will not cut in half after the second user, and,
depending on the badnwidth (or functional size) of the connection, will take
several connections without bogging down.  Examples of this type of connection
are:  56k line, 128k line, T1 line, T3 line.  A T1 will supply over 16
connections with little slowdown. a T3 will supply more than most people need.

@X0BWHAT YOU NEED
-------------
@X03Using a SLIP or PPP connection, you'll need software to call your provider and
etablish a connection.  This software is not supplied by Clark Development,
but you may find some on our Support BBS.  KSP is an example.

Using a digital line, you will need some basic hardware.  A CSU/DSU unit and a
router are necessary for most features.  A router needs to be networked using
an ethernet connection in most cases.  This is your basic hardware.
Descriptions below require ethernet-type network unless otherwise specified.
 
@X0AISDN
----
@X03Requires 128k line or faster.  Connect a digital modem to your router, then run
it into your com ports.  Does not require network.
 
NOTE: Some ISDN modems have a built-in router, and don't require a separate
router.
 
